Here is the second "friendly user" release of my TNC code for the Xerox
820. The code is mostly in C and was developed with the Aztec C compiler.
There are two ready-to-run COM files on this disk, but I'd strongly
recommend that you acquire Aztec C. It will make it much easier to
configure or modify the code, or to incorporate bug fixes.

The major change from the first release is inclusion of support for the
FADCA 8530 daughter board. Most of the files remain the same; to make a
version for the FAD board you just replace 820.c, 820.h and line.c with
fad.c and fad.h. Note: I developed the FAD code using an early version of
the FAD board which did not have its own crystal; the Xerox 820 processor
clock was used instead. Since I have not yet built up a copy of the newer
FAD board, I have not tested the compile-time options which allow use of the
onboard crystal. The binary file here is for the older board. From comparing
the schematics of the two boards, I believe that it should still work on
the newer board, ignoring the crystal. But again, I haven't tested this,
so some reports from users trying out the new board would be welcome.

I renamed the configuration file "CONFIG.TNC" to "PARAM.PAD". By
coincidence, Hank, W0RLI picked the same name out of the air for his PBBS
configuration file and since he got there first, I wanted to avoid any
confusion.

A compatibility problem with the Big Board (an 820 look-alike) was
also resolved. This code should run as-is on the Big Board.

Two executable files are provided: TNCSIO and TNCFAD. Use the first
version when using the SIO on the 820 board; use the latter when running
with the FAD board. It's important to run either program from the currently
logged-in disk, since the program will attempt to read a file called
"PARAM.PAD" and it must find it on the current drive.

This version of the code supports only the keyboard and memory-mapped
display as the console, and uses both channels of the SIO or 8530 for HDLC
ports. If you are using the SIO, you must of course have added the necessary
clock recovery/NRZI generator circuit, and in either case you need an external
modem and radio. The RTS line from the SIO or FAD RS-232 port should key the
transmitter; the modem must also return CTS before the TNC will actually begin
a frame. Carrier detect from the modem must also be provided if CSMA
(carrier-sense multiple-access) operation is desired, which is the usual case.
A watchdog timer on the transmitter key line is also HIGHLY recommended.

You can customize the PARAM.PAD file for your station with the various
commands, followed by a "save" command so that you don't have to do this
each time the program is run.

The file 'USAGE.DOC' gives detailed instructions for each command. The
file 'DESCRIPT.DOC' documents the internal layout and implementation
details of the code itself.

And now for the commercial.

It would be very nice if you'd support two organizations which have done so
much to make all this fun possible: AMSAT and TAPR. While I don't personally
want any money for this code, you should honestly consider how much this
program would have cost you had you bought it from one of the "for-profit"
amateur radio software houses, and make a comparable donation to one of these
non-profit organizations. You will feel VERY GUILTY if you do not do this.
Remember, donations to AMSAT and TAPR are tax-deductible, and will do much
to bring you the satellites and terrestrial hardware which YOU will use in
the amateur packet radio networks of the future.  Thank you.

